1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,750
The male drops in from above,
and the gooseneck seats.

2
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:15,190
This time we mark it out and cut it, step
by step, following one training course.

3
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:19,640
That course's exercise
adds a small stub tenon

4
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:22,770
at the seat, to stop the pieces twisting.

5
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:26,930
We'll leave it out and follow
the steps for the joint itself.

6
00:00:32,333 --> 00:00:35,423
First, mark which piece is which.

7
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:39,453
The male is the upper
piece; on a sill, the

8
00:00:39,493 --> 00:00:42,083
anchor bolt goes in it, beside the splice.

9
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:47,577
Both pieces already carry
center lines, as in episode one.

10
00:00:48,007 --> 00:00:51,677
The neck and head are laid out from them.

11
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:57,943
On the female, the course
marks the end line first.

12
00:00:58,473 --> 00:01:05,063
Then the seat lines, the lines for the
neck and the head's tip, and the cut line.

13
00:01:05,333 --> 00:01:08,663
The neck lines are split
evenly from the center line.

14
00:01:09,253 --> 00:01:13,233
The course notes that a
sashigane's blade is about fifteen

15
00:01:13,333 --> 00:01:17,023
millimeters wide, which makes
these lines quick to mark.

16
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:20,487
The head's slanted lines
also use the blade's

17
00:01:20,527 --> 00:01:24,197
width, running from the
tip toward the neck.

18
00:01:25,287 --> 00:01:29,457
Then the seat lines are
carried down both side faces.

19
00:01:33,967 --> 00:01:36,597
The male is marked the same way:

20
00:01:37,167 --> 00:01:43,127
the end line, the neck and tip lines and
the cut line, the neck lines from the

21
00:01:43,167 --> 00:01:48,937
center, the slanted head lines,
and the seat lines down both sides.

22
00:01:52,133 --> 00:01:53,603
Cutting the female.

23
00:01:54,553 --> 00:01:58,663
Cut the end at the end line
and mark the end grain.

24
00:01:59,613 --> 00:02:05,543
Rip in, leaving half the line, angling
the saw so it doesn't damage the seat.

25
00:02:06,593 --> 00:02:11,243
Then crosscut, leaving half
the line, to form the seat.

26
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:16,097
Chop the head's tip straight
down, taking the line.

27
00:02:17,107 --> 00:02:21,647
Then chop along both sides,
using the saw kerfs as a

28
00:02:21,687 --> 00:02:25,357
guide, and clear out the
socket from the side.

29
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:31,120
The course digs a little past
the line at the tip, about three

30
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:37,050
millimeters, and checks that the
floor is flat and level with the seat.

31
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:42,583
Along the head's sides, it
chops leaving half the line.

32
00:02:43,173 --> 00:02:46,993
Where the head meets the
neck, it stops about three

33
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:51,313
millimeters short of the line,
on the tip side, then pares

34
00:02:51,353 --> 00:02:54,943
that strip into a slight
slope on the bearing faces.

35
00:02:59,233 --> 00:03:01,063
Cutting the male.

36
00:03:01,673 --> 00:03:06,353
Cut the end at the end line
and mark the end grain.

37
00:03:07,013 --> 00:03:11,208
Rip in along the face that
will rest on the seat, then

38
00:03:11,248 --> 00:03:14,983
crosscut the shoulder,
leaving half the line on both.

39
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:19,600
Rip the head's sides down
to the neck, leaving the

40
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:23,170
line at the top and
taking it at the bottom.

41
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,680
The head is a hair narrower below, so it

42
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:31,370
enters easily and gets
snug as it goes down.

43
00:03:31,860 --> 00:03:36,540
Crosscut the shoulder, stopping
short of the neck line, and

44
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:40,530
the waste beside the head
splits off with the chisel.

45
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:45,670
Saw the bearing slope,
leaving half the line.

46
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,640
Add kerfs about a chisel's
width apart beside the neck, and

47
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:55,910
chop the neck, leaving half
the line, with the same taper.

48
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:00,640
Finally, pare the bearing
slope, and chamfer

49
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:03,310
the bottom edges of the head and neck.

50
00:04:07,433 --> 00:04:11,643
Then the male drops in
from above, onto the seat.

51
00:04:14,833 --> 00:04:15,833
Next time:

52
00:04:16,253 --> 00:04:19,113
the other splice the
house specification from

53
00:04:19,153 --> 00:04:22,463
episode nine offers for
the outer-wall beams.

54
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:25,283
Center lines first.

55
00:04:26,133 --> 00:04:30,373
Mark both pieces the same
way, split the neck from the

56
00:04:30,413 --> 00:04:35,063
center, and cut a slight taper
so the head enters easily.

57
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:46,343
One note:

58
00:04:46,433 --> 00:04:51,853
sharp-tool technique, and how
to use these tools safely, is

59
00:04:51,893 --> 00:04:56,263
best learned from a teacher,
at a bench, in person.
